t, we will detail the basic ideas and principles of modeling for these two decision tree models, ID3 and C4.5. The basic modeling process of ID3 and C4.5 is similar to that of the CART tree, as both select the best dataset partitioning method based on purity evaluation metrics. However, ID3 and C4.5 use information entropy as the evaluation metric, and the partitioning method for discrete features in the dataset involves expanding one column at a time, rather than finding split points for partitioning. We will start by introducing the basic principles of ID3, followed by discussing the improvements made by C4.5 on the foundation of ID3.\n\n1. Basic Modeling Process of the ID3 Decision Tree\nID3 is a decision tree model that can only classify problems using discrete variables. In other words, ID3 cannot handle continuous features or regression problems. If the training data contains continuous variables, they must first be discretized, i.e., continuous variable binning. For example, in the following personal consumption data, all features are discrete variables. It can be seen that the \"age\" and \"income\" columns are the results of binning continuous variables, where \"age\" is binned using 30 and 40 as boundaries. Of course, in addition to the representation below, we can also assign discrete values such as 1, 2, or 3 to the binned results.\n\nMore methods for discretizing continuous variables will be introduced in the feature engineering section.\n\nThe growth process of ID3 is essentially the same as that of the CART tree, with the goal of reducing dataset impurity as much as possible. The growth process is also a continuous partitioning of the dataset. However, the dataset partitioning process (rule extraction process) of ID3 differs from that of the CART tree. The CART tree searches for split points across all features and then selects the partitioning method that can most effectively reduce dataset impurity. In other words, the CART tree expands based on a s