, C4.5 can still only solve classification problems; it remains essentially a classification tree. Next, we will discuss the specific improvement strategies of C4.5 in detail.\n\nInformation Value (IV)\nIn C4.5, information value (referred to as IV) is an indicator used to measure the number of branches when partitioning a dataset. The more branches there are during partitioning, the higher the IV value. The specific formula for calculating IV is as follows:\n\nThe IV calculation formula is essentially the same as the information entropy formula, except that the proportions used are no longer the proportions of different classes of samples but the proportions of data in each partitioned child node. In other words, information entropy measures the disorder of different label values, while IV measures the disorder of different feature values.\n\nHere, K represents the total number of branches in a partition, $v_i$ represents a sample after partitioning, and $P(v_i)$ represents the proportion of that sample's size relative to the parent node's total data size. For the following three dataset partitioning scenarios, the IV values are simply calculated:\n\n# Parent node partitioned 50%-50%\n- (1/2 * np.log2(1/2) + 1/2 * np.log2(1/2))\n1.0\n\n# Parent node partitioned 1/4-1/2-1/4\n- (1/4 * np.log2(1/4) + 1/2 * np.log2(1/2) + 1/4 * np.log2(1/4))\n1.5\n\n# Parent node partitioned 1/4-1/4-1/4-1/4\n- (1/4 * np.log2(1/4) + 1/4 * np.log2(1/4) + 1/4 * np.log2(1/4) + 1/4 * np.log2(1/4))\n2.0\n\nIn actual modeling, ID3 selects partitioning rules based on the calculation results of information gain, while C4.5 uses IV to adjust the information gain calculation results, constructing a new dataset partitioning evaluation metric: the gain ratio (GR), to guide the selection of specific partitioning rules. The formula for GR is as follows:\n\nIn other words, during the modeling process of C4.5, GR must first be calculated, and then the column with the larger GR result is selected for expansion. For example, in the above example, the information gain (IG) after expanding the \"age\" column is:\nIG = ent_A - ent_B\nIG\n0.24674981977443922\n\nThe IV value is:\nIV = - (5/14 * np.log2(5/14) + 5/14 * np.log2(5/14) + 4/14 * np.log2(4/14))\nIV\n1.5774062828523454\n\nThus, the GR value is calculated as:\nGR = IG / IV\nGR\n", "\" column is:\nIG = ent_A - ent_B\nIG\n0.24674981977443922\n\nThe IV value is:\nIV = - (5/14 * np.log2(5/14) + 5/14 * np.log2(5/14) + 4/14 * np.log2(4/14))\nIV\n1.5774062828523454\n\nThus, the GR value is calculated as:\nGR = IG / IV\nGR\n0.1564275624211752\n\nNext, the GR values for expanding other columns are calculated, and the column with the larger GR value is selected for dataset partitioning.\n\nHandling Continuous Variables in C4.5\nC4.5 allows the inclusion of continuous variables for modeling, and the rule extraction method for continuous variables is the same as that introduced earlier for the CART tree. That is, the midpoints of adjacent values in continuous variables are used as candidate split points, and the GR values after splitting are calculated to determine the final dataset partitioning method.\n\nOf course, the candidate splitting methods based on continuous variables must also be compared horizontally with the splitting methods for discrete variables. Whether to expand a discrete column once or split based on a certain point in a continuous variable depends on the final GR calculation results.\n\nFor example, if the \"age\" column in the above dataset is replaced with a continuous variable, the GR scenarios to be calculated become GR(income), GR(student), GR(credit_rating), GR(age<=26.5), GR(age<=27.5), etc.\n\nSince the rule extraction methods for discrete and continuous variables in C4.5 differ—discrete variables consume one column at a time for expansion (potentially multi-branch), while continuous variables consume one split point at a time—the same continuous variable can guide dataset partitioning multiple 